{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01WUe21hJT9S2vx6ACCaStdu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: \"api:scaffold\"\n3\tdescription: \"Generate a REST API endpoint with handler, route, and basic tests. Use when adding new API endpoints to the project.\"\n4\targument-hint: \"<resource-name> [--method GET|POST|PUT|DELETE]\"\n5\tallowed-tools: [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Bash\"]\n6\tuser-invocable: true\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t# API Endpoint Scaffolder\n10\t\n11\tCreate a new REST API endpoint with handler function, route registration, request/response types, and a basic test file.\n12\t\n13\t## Steps\n14\t\n15\t### Step 1: Detect Project Framework\n16\t\n17\tRead the project's dependency file to determine which web framework is in use:\n18\t- Express.js / Fastify / Koa (Node.js)\n19\t- Gin / Chi / Echo (Go)\n20\t- FastAPI / Flask / Django (Python)\n21\t\n22\t### Step 2: Generate Handler\n23\t\n24\tCreate the handler file following the project's existing patterns. Look at existing handlers for:\n25\t- File naming conventions\n26\t- Import style\n27\t- Error handling patterns\n28\t- Response format\n29\t\n30\t### Step 3: Register Route\n31\t\n32\tAdd the route to the project's router configuration. Match the existing route registration style.\n33\t\n34\t### Step 4: Create Types\n35\t\n36\tDefine request and response types/structs for the endpoint.\n37\t\n38\t### Step 5: Generate Test\n39\t\n40\tCreate a basic test file with:\n41\t- A test for the happy path (200 response)\n42\t- A test for invalid input (400 response)\n43\t- A test for not found (404 response)\n44\t\n45\t### Step 6: Verify\n46\t\n47\tRun the project's test suite to confirm the new tests pass and nothing is broken.\n48\t\n49\t## Notes\n50\t\n51\t- The generated code follows existing project conventions\n52\t- Review the generated handler logic before using in production\n53\t- Complex business logic should be added manually after scaffolding\n54\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"22319cfd-eae7-4964-9395-311733a2b644","uuid":"73b42f1b-98f3-4969-8eaf-2b1443dccbae","timestamp":"2026-06-01T13:17:01.456Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/agent-eval/2026-06-01-opus/cases/case-002-present-to-strong/target-skill/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: \"api:scaffold\"\ndescription: \"Generate a REST API endpoint with handler, route, and basic tests. Use when adding new API endpoints to the project.\"\nargument-hint: \"<resource-name> [--method GET|POST|PUT|DELETE]\"\nallowed-tools: [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Bash\"]\nuser-invocable: true\n---\n\n# API Endpoint Scaffolder\n\nCreate a new REST API endpoint with handler function, route registration, request/response types, and a basic test file.\n\n## Steps\n\n### Step 1: Detect Project Framework\n\nRead the project's dependency file to determine which web framework is in use:\n- Express.js / Fastify / Koa (Node.js)\n- Gin / Chi / Echo (Go)\n- FastAPI / Flask / Django (Python)\n\n### Step 2: Generate Handler\n\nCreate the handler file following the project's existing patterns. Look at existing handlers for:\n- File naming conventions\n- Import style\n- Error handling patterns\n- Response format\n\n### Step 3: Register Route\n\nAdd the route to the project's router configuration. Match the existing route registration style.\n\n### Step 4: Create Types\n\nDefine request and response types/structs for the endpoint.\n\n### Step 5: Generate Test\n\nCreate a basic test file with:\n- A test for the happy path (200 response)\n- A test for invalid input (400 response)\n- A test for not found (404 response)\n\n### Step 6: Verify\n\nRun the project's test suite to confirm the new tests pass and nothing is broken.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The generated code follows existing project conventions\n- Review the generated handler logic before using in production\n- Complex business logic should be added manually after scaffolding\n","numLines":54,"startLine":1,"totalLines":54}}}
