{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01Fnnggfz6gNsh5RfMpQ1oaY", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Both files loaded. Now evaluating the skill against all 14 patterns and preparing the enhancement.\n\n### Steps Executed\n- Step 1: Input Handling \u2014 completed\n- Step 2: Load Knowledge \u2014 completed\n- Step 3: Baseline Evaluation \u2014 completed\n- Step 4: Already-Optimal Detection \u2014 not optimal, continued\n- Step 5: Enhancement Targeting \u2014 completed, 12 targets selected (5 absent, 7 present)\n- Step 6: Rewrite \u2014 completed\n- Step 7: File Splitting \u2014 skipped (under 500 lines)\n- Step 8: Prompt Plugin Delegation \u2014 skipped (plugin not available)\n- Step 9: Changelog \u2014 in progress\n\n## Enhanced Skill\n\n```markdown\n---\nname: \"api:scaffold\"\ndescription: \"Generate a REST API endpoint with handler, route registration, request/response types, and basic tests. Use when adding, creating, or scaffolding new API endpoints, routes, or resources in the project. Do NOT use for modifying existing endpoints, adding middleware, or non-REST interfaces (GraphQL, gRPC, WebSocket).\"\nargument-hint: \"<resource-name> [--method GET|POST|PUT|DELETE]\"\nallowed-tools: [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Bash\"]\nuser-invocable: true\n---\n\n# API Endpoint Scaffolder\n\nCreate a new REST API endpoint with handler function, route registration, request/response types, and a basic test file.\n\n**Example**: `/api:scaffold orders --method POST` creates a POST `/orders` endpoint, producing files like:\n\n```\nsrc/handlers/orders.ts       # Handler with createOrder function\nsrc/types/orders.ts          # CreateOrderRequest / CreateOrderResponse types\nsrc/routes/index.ts          # Modified: new POST /orders route entry\nsrc/handlers/orders.test.ts  # Tests for 200, 400, 404 cases\n```\n\nActual paths and naming follow the project's existing conventions. If the target handler file already exists, report the conflict and stop rather than overwriting.\n\n## Steps\n\n### Step 1: Detect Project Framework\n\nRead the project's root dependency file (`package.json`, `go.mod`, `requirements.txt`, or `pyproject.toml`) to determine which web framework is in use:\n- Express.js / Fastify / Koa (Node.js)\n- Gin / Chi / Echo (Go)\n- FastAPI / Flask / Django (Python)\n\nIf no recognized framework is found, stop and report: \"Could not detect a supported web framework. Verify the project has a dependency file with a supported framework.\"\n\n### Step 2: Analyze Existing Patterns\n\nRead up to 2 existing handler files to learn the project's conventions for:\n- File naming and directory structure\n- Import style\n- Error handling patterns\n- Response format (JSON envelope structure, status codes)\n\nLimit to 2 files to keep context focused on patterns, not content. If no existing handlers are found, use framework defaults.\n\n### Step 3: Present Scaffold Plan\n\nBefore creating any files, present the user with the planned changes:\n\n- Each file to be created (with full paths)\n- Each file to be modified (with the specific change)\n- The route path and HTTP method\n\nWait for user approval before proceeding. The scaffold creates and modifies multiple files at once, so confirming the plan prevents misplaced files or naming mismatches.\n\n### Step 4: Generate Handler\n\nCreate the handler file following the conventions from Step 2. Match the existing code style exactly.\n\nMake sure the handler function signature matches what the router expects. A common mistake is scaffolding signatures for one framework style (e.g., `(req, res)`) while the project uses another (e.g., middleware-chain `(ctx)`). Compare against the existing handlers from Step 2 before writing.\n\n### Step 5: Create Types and Register Route\n\n1. Define request and response types/structs for the endpoint.\n2. Add the route to the project's router configuration, matching the existing registration style.\n\nCheck for route conflicts before adding. Duplicate or overlapping route patterns (e.g., `/users/:id` vs `/users/new`) cause silent routing bugs that are difficult to trace at runtime.\n\n### Step 6: Generate Tests\n\nCreate a test file with:\n- A test for the happy path (200 response)\n- A test for invalid input (400 response)\n- A test for not found (404 response)\n\nThese three cases cover the most common failure modes for new endpoints while keeping the scaffold minimal enough to extend.\n\n### Step 7: Verify\n\nRun the project's test suite to confirm all tests pass.\n\nIf tests fail, read the error output and fix the generated code. Retry up to 2 times. If tests still fail after 2 retries, report the remaining errors to the user and stop.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Review the generated handler logic before using in production\n- Complex business logic should be added manually after scaffolding\n```\n\n---\n\n## What Changed\n\n| Pattern Applied | What was added/changed | Why |\n|----------------|----------------------|-----|\n| Activation Metadata | Rewrote description with trigger verbs (\"adding, creating, scaffolding\") and exclusion clause inline | Original description was functional but lacked routing-rule precision; Claude now has clearer activation signals |\n| Exclusion Clause | Added \"Do NOT use for modifying existing endpoints, adding middleware, or non-REST interfaces (GraphQL, gRPC, WebSocket)\" to description | No exclusions existed; most likely misroutes are editing existing endpoints or scaffolding non-REST interfaces |\n| Context Budget | Step 2 now caps handler reads to 2 files with rationale; Step 1 names specific dependency files | Original said \"Look at existing handlers\" without scope limits, risking large context loads in projects with many endpoints |\n| Control Tuning | Step 1 has a halt condition; Step 3 is a mandatory gate; Step 4 uses imperative \"Match exactly\"; Step 7 specifies retry count | Original had uniform specificity across all steps, making it unclear which parts were strict vs. flexible |\n| Explain-the-Why | Added rationale to: Step 2 file limit, Step 3 approval gate, Step 6 test case selection | Original instructions were bare imperatives; Claude could not judge edge cases without understanding intent |\n| Template Scaffold | Added output structure example showing generated file paths with descriptions | Original described output in prose only; the filled example shows exactly what the scaffold produces |\n| In-Skill Examples | Added invocation example (`/api:scaffold orders --method POST`) with expected output and conflict edge case | No examples existed; input-to-output mapping was non-obvious (resource name to file paths and naming) |\n| Known Gotchas | Step 4: handler signature mismatch across framework styles; Step 5: route conflict detection | API scaffolding has real pitfalls (wrong signature style, duplicate routes) that the original did not warn about |\n| Execution Checklist | Step 1 halts on missing framework; Step 3 gates on approval; Step 7 has retry limit and explicit stop | Steps were numbered but lacked halt/completion criteria, so failures could cascade silently |\n| Self-Correcting Loop | Step 7 now mandates test run, defines retry limit (2), and specifies failure behavior (report and stop) | Original mentioned running tests but had no retry logic or failure path |\n| Plan-Validate-Execute | New Step 3 requires presenting the scaffold plan and waiting for user approval | Original jumped straight into file creation; scaffolding multiple files without preview risks misplaced files |\n| Autonomy Calibration | Approval gate in Step 3 now matches the skill's risk profile (multi-file creation/modification) | Tool restrictions existed but lacked an approval gate despite the skill modifying project files |\n\n## Status Change\n\n| Pattern | Before | After |\n|---------|--------|-------|\n| Activation Metadata | present | strong |\n| Exclusion Clause | absent | strong |\n| Context Budget | present | strong |\n| Control Tuning | present | strong |\n| Explain-the-Why | absent | strong |\n| Template Scaffold | present | strong |\n| In-Skill Examples | absent | strong |\n| Known Gotchas | absent | strong |\n| Execution Checklist | present | strong |\n| Self-Correcting Loop | present | strong |\n| Plan-Validate-Execute | absent | strong |\n| Autonomy Calibration | present | strong |\n\n**Summary**: 12 patterns improved (5 absent to strong, 7 present to strong), 0 new files proposed"}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4982, "cache_read_input_tokens": 45693, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 4982, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "22319cfd-eae7-4964-9395-311733a2b644", "uuid": "d492131d-5405-42e8-ab62-e38cc810a76b", "request_id": "req_vrtx_011CbcbydkjWQuT2dMSy98CZ", "timestamp": "2026-06-01T13:20:18.509Z"}
